# CHAPTER III

## VIOLENCE

#### Article 5

1. Forms of violence against women and children include:  
a. physical violence;  
b. psychological violence;  
c. sexual violence;  
d. neglect;  
e. exploitation; and/or
f. other violence.  
2. Physical violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is caused by acts that result in pain, injury, wounds or disability to a person's body, miscarriage, unconsciousness and/or cause death.  
3. Psychological violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b is caused by acts that result in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and/or severe psychological suffering to a person.  
4. Sexual violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c is caused by:  
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;  
b. forced sexual intercourse;  
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/or
d. forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.  
5. Neglect as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d is caused by:  
a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill a child's needs properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;  
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after a child as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;  
c. acts of neglecting a person within their household scope, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or  
d. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.  
6. Exploitation as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e is caused by:  
a. acts that exploit economically or sexually with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;  
b. acts with or without the victim's consent that include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, utilization of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully removing or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or exploiting the labor or ability of a person by another party to obtain material or immaterial benefits; and/or  
c. any form of utilization of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain benefits, including but not limited to all prostitution or indecency activities.  
7. Other violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f is caused by:  
a. threats of violence include: any unlawful act in the form of speech, writing, images, symbols, or body movements, whether with or without using means that cause fear or restrict a person's fundamental freedom; and  
b. coercion, includes: a situation where a person/victim is ordered to do something in such a way that the person does something contrary to their own will.

# CHAPTER IV

## RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILD VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

#### Article 6

1. Every woman and child who becomes a victim of physical violence, exploitation or human trafficking is entitled to receive protection and services.  
2. Protection and services for victims as referred to in paragraph (1) include:  
a. complaints or requests for protection from the person concerned;  
b. confidential handling or placement of victims in safe locations and houses whose whereabouts are kept confidential to avoid intimidation and threats;  
c. medical services/health care in the form of treatment and recovery of injuries or physical condition of victims by medical personnel and paramedics;  
d. medicolegal services for evidence in the legal field;  
e. protection by law enforcement officials;  
f. provision of information regarding legal rights and obligations to victims or guardians of victims and identification of incidents;  
g. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;  
h. psychological assistance to restore the traumatic condition of victims and restore victims' self-confidence;  
i. assistance support for legal processes and obtaining compensation or compensation rights;   
j. guarantee of rights related to status as family members and community members (social reintegration); and  
k. economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.  
3. In the event that the victim is a child, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in paragraph (2), they also receive special rights including:  
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;  
b. the right to basic services;  
c. the right to equal protection;  
d. the right to be free from various stigma;  
e. the right to obtain freedom; and  
f. the right to obtain compensation from the perpetrator.

# CHAPTER V

## OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

#### Article 7

Obligations and responsibilities in carrying out prevention and mitigation in the form of protection and services for women and children from acts of violence are the joint responsibility of:  
a. regional government;  
b. community;  
c. family; and  
d. parents.

#### Article 8

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 7 letter a, include:  
a. implementing the obligations of protecting women and children from acts of violence established by the government;  
b. establishing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;  
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;  
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;  
e. providing shelters for women and child victims of violence;  
f. allocating budgets for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence according to regional financial capacity; and  
g. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence.  
2. The implementation of obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), is technically carried out in an integrated manner by related SKPD.

#### Article 9

1. the obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 7 letter b, are carried out in the form of community participation.  
2. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), include:  
a. forming family partners at the sub-district/village level by the community;  
b. forming women and child protection units within community organizations;  
c. conducting socialization of women's and children's rights independently;  
d. providing first aid to victims; and  
e. reporting to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.  
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.  
4. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), are carried out responsibly in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
